  "post": {
    "caption": "In 1772, the French chemist Antoine Lavoisier burned a diamond with a lens that focused sunlight, and the gas it gave off turned limewater cloudy. Then the English chemist Smithson Tennant took the puzzle further. He heated two and a half grains of small diamonds with niter in a gold tube closed at one end, and kept it red hot for about an hour and a half. Only carbon dioxide came off, about the same amount as the same weight of charcoal gives. Tennant concluded that a diamond is charcoal in crystal form, and his paper On the Nature of the Diamond came out in 1797.",
